ation detail and does not define the operating identity.\n\n---\n\n# 4. Initial GCE Stack Subproject — Web Conversation Recovery\n\nThe first active GCE Stack workstream is the existing **Kimi Web Bridge / scraper / conversation-recovery tooling**.\n\nTreat “Kimi” here as the historical/component name of the tool where it already exists.\n\nDo not infer a framework seat named Kimi.\n\nThe purpose of this component is to support:\n\n- historical conversation recovery;\n- export/scraping of chat content;\n- preservation of conversation state;\n- normalization for later ingestion;\n- reliable capture from supported interfaces;\n- backup/recovery workflows.\n\nThis is **support infrastructure**, not the live GCE control plane.\n\nChat scraping/recovery is downstream support work.\n\nIt does not become:\n\n- seat memory authority;\n- live state authority;\n- Command Center authority;\n- framework canon by itself.\n\n---\n\n# 5. Known Existing Technical Context\n\nRelevant known components include:\n\n- Kimi Web Bridge / scraper;\n- local daemon activity;\n- conversation backup/recovery work;\n- prior weekly / multi-day rotation concepts;\n- normalization of recovered chat history;\n- possible status/heartbeat ideas for detecting degraded capture;\n- interaction with local GCE filesystem structure.\n\nDo not assume the current implementation is correct.\n\nFirst establish current truth from the machine/project artifacts.\n\n---\n\n# 6. GCE Stack Project Structure Principle\n\nDo not create a large hierarchy before the work requires it.\n\nStart with a simple parent structure.\n\nSuggested conceptual lanes:\n\n- `00_GOVERNANCE`\n- `01_ACTIVE_SYSTEMS`\n- `02_SHARED_COMPONENTS`\n- `03_DATA_STATE`\n- `04_TEST_EVIDENCE`\n- `05_HANDOFFS`\n- `06_PROCESS_LEARNINGS`\n- `99_ARCHIVE`\n\nThese are **conceptual only until reconciled against the existing filesystem**.\n\nDo not reorganize existing files simply to match this proposed structure.\n\nTruth the current system first.\n\n---\n\n# 7. Web Scraper / Recovery Workstream Questions\n\nThe first project pass should determine:\n\n1. What currently exists?\n2. What is actually live?\n3. What process starts/stops the scraper or daemon?\n4. What sources/interfaces does it capture?\n5. Where does raw recovered content land?\n6. What is transformed or normalized?\n7. What is original evidence vs derived representation?\n8. What state is persistent?\n9. What failure modes exist?\n10. What happens when capture degrades or partially fails?\n11. What files/configs are authoritative?\n12. What historical experiments are stale?\n13. What belongs in GCE Stack vs framework proper?\n14. What is the smallest next useful technical milestone?\n\n---\n\n# 8. Capture Doctrine\n\nPreserve the distinction:\n\n**capture ≠ interpretation**\n\nRaw source evidence should remain recoverable wherever practical.\n\nDerived:\n\n- transcripts;\n- normalized text;\n- summaries;\n- indexes;\n- structured representations\n\nmust not silently replace the original evidence.\n\nThis same principle already proved useful elsewhere in GCE.\n\n---\n\n# 9. Operating Discipline\n\nStart from current implementation truth.\n\nDo not redesign before inspection.\n\nUse:\n\n**OBSERVE → RECONCILE → CLASSIFY → PROPOSE → APPROVE → BUILD → VERIFY**\n\nDo not create layers because the diagram has room.\n\nDo not harden hypothetical failures.\n\nLet real failures expose what needs surgical correction.\n\nOne architectural responsibility at a time.\n\n---\n\n# 10. Routing\n\nFor GCE Stack work:\n\n### Technical premise / readiness problem\n\nRoute to **Vittorio**.\n\n### Material architecture problem\n\nRoute to **Chiave**.\n\n### Implementation / design-to-build\n\nRoute to **Nico**.\n\n### Operations / sequencing\n\nRoute to **Talon**.\n\n### Deployment / service stability\n\nRoute to **Keel**.\n\n### Independent truthing\n\nRoute to **Luce**.\n\n### Data retrieval/capture tasking\n\nRoute to **Falcon** where appropriate.\n\nDo not automatically