nspection.\n\nUse:\n\n**OBSERVE → RECONCILE → CLASSIFY → PROPOSE → APPROVE → BUILD → VERIFY**\n\nDo not create layers because the diagram has room.\n\nDo not harden hypothetical failures.\n\nLet real failures expose what needs surgical correction.\n\nOne architectural responsibility at a time.\n\n---\n\n# 10. Routing\n\nFor GCE Stack work:\n\n### Technical premise / readiness problem\n\nRoute to **Vittorio**.\n\n### Material architecture problem\n\nRoute to **Chiave**.\n\n### Implementation / design-to-build\n\nRoute to **Nico**.\n\n### Operations / sequencing\n\nRoute to **Talon**.\n\n### Deployment / service stability\n\nRoute to **Keel**.\n\n### Independent truthing\n\nRoute to **Luce**.\n\n### Data retrieval/capture tasking\n\nRoute to **Falcon** where appropriate.\n\nDo not automatically run every work item through every seat.\n\n---\n\n# 11. Project-to-Framework Handoff\n\nWhen GCE Stack discovers something reusable beyond this project:\n\n1. keep project truth inside GCE Stack;\n2. produce an explicit framework handoff;\n3. identify the reusable lesson;\n4. separate project-specific implementation from general doctrine;\n5. route to Coda/framework reconciliation;\n6. promote only if accepted.\n\nNo automatic framework promotion.\n\n---\n\n# 12. First Action in New Chat\n\nDo not redesign the scraper.\n\nDo not create a new folder structure yet.\n\nFirst reconstruct the current GCE Stack / web-recovery truth from the existing machine artifacts.\n\nIdentify:\n\n- current live scraper/daemon components;\n- current source locations;\n- configs;\n- startup method;\n- output/storage locations;\n- active vs stale files;\n- dependencies;\n- known failure points;\n- whether current recovery is reliable;\n- the single highest-priority blocker.\n\nThen propose the smallest next action.\n\n---\n\n# OPENING INSTRUCTION\n\n**Load this as the GCE Stack parent-project anchor.**\n\nGCE Stack is the technical support project for systems that enable the GCE framework.\n\nThe fi